{
    "id": 102,
    "title": "Every outside call asks staging's gate; held calls say TEST MODE",
    "why": "decisions/0165 §1 (staging is live in safe mode). Jason, 2026-11-17: \"Gate should be checked right, so we need to update these instances\", and the wording \"TEST MODE: ACTIVE (STAGE REPLIES ONLY)\". The pre-flight's line \"Staging's safe mode: no outside call skips the gate\" found 36 outside calls that skipped StagingGate::hold(). 1. Each now asks the gate first, with a staging reply that fits its caller: sign-in checks (Google, Apple, Microsoft, Facebook) say to sign in with email on staging; cPanel and cPanel mailboxes, GitHub (docs, audit clock), Railway, Amazon SES and SNS (send and change our AWS account) and Textract (paid) are held; payments (QuickBooks, Stripe, Square) reach only the sandbox; public pages (guides' link check, resource checks, the crawler without its switch) are held and leave records as they were; free public data (weather, roads, recalls) is paced; the IP country file and the Census ZIP table are bulk pulls, held; the file store and calls to staging itself go straight through. On live nothing changes: the gate always says go. 2. AWS is 'allow' for the store only: Textract, SES and SNS hosts are held on staging. 3. ServiceHealth::held() asks staging's gate first and on its own, so a failed database can't let a call out of staging; the scan counts it as a gate (it was one already). 4. A held call's message now starts \"TEST MODE: ACTIVE (STAGE REPLIES ONLY)\", then which service wasn't called and why (held, sandbox only, paced), then \"Nothing was sent.\" The ledger's own reason text is unchanged. 5. FAQ: the held notice and what asks the gate.",
